  "summary": "The previous analyses all focus on failure/exception paths but miss a critical happy-path bug: `_run_plan_async` (used for plan-inbox and reviewing-plan items) never calls `_on_plan_done` in any code path — not on success, not on failure, not in its `finally` block. This means plan-inbox and reviewing-plan items are permanently stuck in `_auto_plan_queued` even after completing successfully, not just after errors. Only `_run_ideating_async` calls `_on_plan_done` (and only on success). Additionally, the shared `_rate_limit_until` timestamp (line 3386) gates the entire `_auto_run_check` method (line 3430), meaning a rate limit hit during ideation blocks auto-implement too — not just plan vs ideation as contribution 3 noted. Contribution 3's proposed fix of adding `discard` to `finally` blocks is directionally correct but incomplete: for `_run_plan_async`, the slug is orphaned on ALL paths (not just exceptions), and simply discarding without calling `_on_plan_done` skips necessary UI state cleanup (board refresh, detail view update, notification). The proper fix should: (1) call `_on_plan_done` from the `finally` block of both `_run_plan_async` and `_run_ideating_async` (guarded to avoid double-call on the ideating success path), (2) separate `_rate_limit_until` into per-mode timestamps so rate-limiting plan doesn't stall implement, and (3) consider that the `_plan_running` guard in `_auto_plan_check` (line 3447) already prevents the Textual exclusive-worker cancellation scenario during auto-plan — the cancellation risk only exists from manual user-triggered runs, which is a lower-priority concern."
